\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A surface lamination $\Lambda$ is a closed subset of $M$ locally homeomorphic to a product of a disk with a closed transverse set. It is essential when it has no sphere leaf or torus leaf bounding a solid torus, and its complementary regions are irreducible, have incompressible boundary, and have no compressing monogon. Such a monogon is a compressing disk with one boundary arc on a boundary leaf and the other along a transverse interval fiber. It is genuine if some complementary region is not an interval bundle. A sublamination is a closed union of leaves.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Call an essential lamination very full if every complementary region is an ideal polygon bundle over $S^1$. For a genuine lamination $\Lambda\subset M$, characterize when there exists a very full essential lamination $\Lambda'\supseteq\Lambda$, obtained by adding leaves in the complementary regions. Determine whether requiring $M$ to be hyperbolic improves existence or its characterization. Coorientation of $\Lambda'$ is not an imposed hypothesis. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0209081v1}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine when a genuine lamination can be enlarged so that every complementary region is an ideal polygon bundle, and how hyperbolicity affects this.

